---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="About" current="about" description="Models, chart symbols, heights and forecast updates.">
  <h1>About Windgrams</h1>
  <div class="stack" style="margin-top:20px">
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Choose a forecast</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Pick a launch, model, day and hour. Strongest thermals selects the hour with the highest forecast thermal strength.
        The windgram fits the full day on a phone; tap a column for that hour's conditions and sounding.
        Choose Imperial or Metric in the header. Your selection is saved in this browser.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Choose United States or Armenia on the launch map. Armenia uses ICON-EU, with GFS and available global models for comparison.
        HRRR and HRDPS cover the next two days. RRFS extends to 84 hours; RDPS adds another regional forecast.
        GFS and GDPS show the longer trend. GEPS shows the median and spread of 21 ensemble members.
        The Soaring map offers HRRR and RRFS for North Sound and Shenandoah–Blue Ridge, and ICON-EU for Armenia.
        ICON-EU resolves weather at roughly 6.5 km; narrow valleys, ridge exposure and launch gusts remain local effects.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Read the windgram</h2>
      <ul style="margin-top:8px">
        <li><strong>Background:</strong> temperature change with height, using <a href="http://wxtofly.net/windgramexplain.html">WxToFly's color scale</a>. Red and orange show rapid cooling; gray shows an inversion.</li>
        <li><strong>Lift w*:</strong> forecast convective strength. Stronger values suggest stronger thermals; climb rates vary within them.</li>
        <li><strong>Wind barbs:</strong> wind direction and speed. Feathers point toward the source of the wind.</li>
        <li><strong>Paraglider:</strong> the ceiling for a <span data-unit="lift" data-value="1.143">225 ft/min</span> sink rate. The blue line uses <span data-unit="lift" data-value="1">197 ft/min</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>Outlined cloud:</strong> potential condensation height. White crosshatching marks humidity above 94%; pale bands show model cloud.</li>
        <li><strong>Temperature lines:</strong> equal temperature across the day. Snowflakes mark the lowest freezing level.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">A higher potential cloud base can occur as thermals weaken. Compare thermal strength, usable height and wind for the same hour.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Heights and soundings</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Chart heights are above mean sea level (MSL). The launch line comes from a terrain elevation estimate at the
        catalogued coordinates. Model terrain is smoother and can sit above or below the launch. Vegetation and buildings
        can affect surface-model elevations.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        The model sounding shows temperature, moisture and wind through the selected forecast column. Balloon soundings
        describe the nearby upper-air station; its name and distance appear with the chart. Times show the local time zone
        and the nominal UTC observation cycle. Morning thermal estimates use the day's NWS forecast high when available.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Updates and other guidance</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Forecast refreshes start at 01:55, 07:55, 13:55 and 19:55 UTC. RRFS starts two hours later; ICON-EU starts at 04:55, 10:55, 16:55 and 22:55 UTC.
        Run labels show the forecast currently published; delayed feeds retain their last complete run.
        US pages include NWS forecasts, discussions and nearby balloon observations. ECMWF guidance adds a global comparison.
        Connected station panels show timestamped measurements. Local terrain, exposure and changing weather also shape launch conditions.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section class="card">
      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Models: <a href="https://opendata.dwd.de/weather/nwp/v1/m/icon-eu/p/">DWD ICON-EU</a>
        (<a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>), <a href="https://www.noaa.gov/">NOAA</a> and
        <a href="https://eccc-msc.github.io/open-data/licence/readme_en/">Environment and Climate Change Canada</a>,
        processed with <a href="https://meteo.azohra.com/">meteo by Azohra</a>.
        Official forecasts: <a href="https://www.weather.gov/">National Weather Service</a>.
        ECMWF IFS: © <a href="https://www.ecmwf.int/">ECMWF</a>,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>, via <a href="https://open-meteo.com/">Open-Meteo</a>.
        Balloons: NWS via the <a href="https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/archive/raob/">Iowa Environmental Mesonet</a>.
        Station feeds: <a href="https://www.wunderground.com/">Weather Underground</a>. Launch catalogue: flyingsites.
        Armenian launch data: <a href="https://paraglidingearth.com/">ParaglidingEarth contributors</a>,
        <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/">CC BY-SA 3.0</a>.
        Additional site research: <a href="https://skyclub.am/paragliding-guided-tours/flying-sites/">Sky Club</a>.
        Source links appear in Launch info.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-top:8px">
        Launch terrain uses Copernicus GLO-30, GLO-90 in Armenia, and available regional elevation data. Sunlight terrain uses
        <a href="https://www.usgs.gov/3d-elevation-program">USGS 3DEP</a> with a Copernicus fallback.
        Produced using Copernicus WorldDEM-30 and WorldDEM-90 © DLR e.V. 2010–2014 and © Airbus Defence and Space GmbH 2014–2018,
        provided under COPERNICUS by the European Union and ESA; all rights reserved.
        Land cover: © ESA WorldCover project 2021 / Contains modified Copernicus Sentinel data (2021),
        processed by ESA WorldCover consortium, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>.
        Regional elevation products contain information licensed under the
        <a href="https://open.canada.ca/en/open-government-licence-canada">Open Government Licence – Canada</a>
        and the <a href="https://www2.gov.bc.ca/gov/content/data/open-data/open-government-licence-bc">Open Government Licence – British Columbia</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
